eoplasm", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.001, "danger": 0.05, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Congenital tricuspid stenosis with stable growth parameters strongly favors a systemic developmental disorder (B2) over isolated respiratory infection (B1). Th…"]}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2"], "other_mass": 0.0, "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Chronic Lower Respiratory Tract Infection? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "The evidence most strongly supports an inborn error with multisystem respiratory and cardiac involvement while significantly arguing against a chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm. The MRI airway dilation and the combination of congenital tricuspid stenosis with lifelong symptoms and growth patterns…"}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the MRI finding of airway dilation (consistent with bronchiectasis) argue for a chronic lower respiratory tract infection etiology given the lifelong respiratory symptoms?", "summary": "MRI findings of bronchiectasis (airway dilation) are weakly supportive of a chronic infection but more robustly indicate an inborn error given the lifelong pattern. The congenital tricuspid stenosis further supports a multisystem genetic disorder."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of tricuspid stenosis (a congenital cardiac anomaly) combined with normal growth metrics (25th percentile since birth) contradict a purely respiratory tract infection diagnosis?", "summary": "Congenital tricuspid stenosis with stable growth parameters strongly favors a systemic developmental disorder (B2) over isolated respiratory infection (B1). The chronic sinus and airway findings are more consistent with an underlying syndrome."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the MRI showing airway dilation (bronchiectasis) in a patient with persistent mucus production since birth strongly argue for a chronic respiratory infection etiology?", "summary": "MRI findings of airway dilation provide only weak support for a chronic infection (LR+2.3) but are more consistent with a multisystem inborn error."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "How does the finding of congenital tricuspid stenosis with stable linear growth metrics specifically support an inborn error with multisystem features?", "summary": "The coexistence of a structural cardiac defect with stable growth pattern significantly supports a congenital developmental disorder (strong_for) rather than an infection (neutral impact)."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could congenital tricuspid stenosis and stable growth metrics (25th percentile since birth) potentially rule out an isolated respiratory tract infection etiology?", "summary": "This combination provides moderate clinical exclusion of an isolated respiratory infection (LR-0.35) while reinforcing the inborn error hypothesis."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.645], ["B2", 0.351], ["B3", 0.004]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.504], ["B2", 0.494], ["B3", 0.002]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability detected.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "B1's posterior (0.504) is below 0.75. B2's danger (0.5) is below the 0.7 threshold for dangerous alternatives, but sweat chloride testing (option C) represents a high-value unresolved discriminator to distinguish inborn errors from chronic infection."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.85, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.5042644586938929, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following findings is associated with this patient's most likely underlying d